    "final_answer": "I have successfully completed the task using Wolfram Alpha. Here are the results:\n\n## Results\n\n### 1. Conversion of 15 kg of Sulfuric Acid (H₂SO₄) to Moles\n**15 kg sulfuric acid = 153 mol** (≈ 152.95 mol)\n\nThis was calculated using the molar mass of sulfuric acid:\n- M(H₂SO₄) = 2(1.008) + 32.06 + 4(16.00) = 98.08 g/mol\n- n = 15,000 g ÷ 98.08 g/mol ≈ 153 mol\n\n### 2. Percentage Composition by Mass of Sulfuric Acid (H₂SO₄)\n\n| Element | Symbol | Mass Percentage |\n|---------|--------|----------------|\n| Hydrogen | H | **2.056%** |\n| Sulfur | S | **32.69%** |\n| Oxygen | O | **65.25%** |\n| **Total** | | **100%** |\n\nThese results were obtained by submitting two queries to Wolfram Alpha:\n1. `15 kg sulfuric acid to moles` → returned 153 mol\n2. `percentage composition of sulfuric acid H2SO4 by mass` → returned the mass percentages of H, S, and O as shown in the table above (Wolfram Alpha also provided a pie chart visualization of the mass fractions).",
